## 6. Scenario rationale

**Controlled Pullback.** Protects Bing, the only channel with a proven and competitive cost per submitted application at scale. Holds Azerion at its current weight rather than cutting it outright, until the full August vendor file settles what that weight actually is. Retires the superseded Quantcast campaign and holds the remaining productive Quantcast line at half its own weight, so a modelled, retargetable audience stays live and can be scaled quickly rather than rebuilt from zero. Removes Native, which reports no attributed applications and is the only line currently spending its full approved budget. The cost of keeping a live upper-funnel audience is about $10,379 a month.

**Deep Pullback.** Keeps only Bing. It removes all reach spend and also cuts Azerion. That cut is conditional on the full August vendor file: on July evidence alone, at about $915 per application against Bing's $237, Azerion is the weaker line, but the August read is not yet available to confirm it. It preserves the most cash and accepts the cost and time of restarting upper-funnel activity from cold. It is the stronger choice if cash preservation becomes the sole priority, or if the September read does not improve the Quantcast line.

**A decision on Bing that sits inside both scenarios.** Both models hold Bing at $18,533, its current pace, which leaves $11,467 a month of approved Bing budget unspent. That is deliberately conservative while funding reads soft. It is worth stating plainly that if FOREX.com wants applications rather than savings, the most defensible use of money in this program is funding Bing toward its already-approved $30,000, because it is the only line where the cost per application is both known and competitive. That is a separate decision from the pullback and we are not folding it into either scenario.

**Quantcast is the genuine swing.** The retained line carries a retargetable, modelled audience, and its productive campaigns began returning result signals in August where the native segment had shown none in July. Against that, current Quantcast results are recorded as view-through, click attribution is not yet confirmed, and the retargeting segment is a thin and expensive share of delivery. The Controlled Pullback therefore holds Quantcast as a monitored option rather than a proven performer. If the September read does not show its cost per result and attribution quality improving, the correct move is the Deep Pullback and a full cut.

