## 8. On the stablecoin launch

Stablecoin adoption in Latin America is real and substantial. Regional crypto activity has grown sharply, stablecoins represent more than half of exchange purchases in several important markets, and research from the Bank for International Settlements describes a large share of fiat-to-stablecoin conversion originating outside the US dollar. We would not tell FOREX.com that stablecoins do not matter here.

The narrower and better-supported statement is this: **stablecoin adoption does not automatically translate into funded FOREX.com trading accounts.** The dominant use cases in the region are dollar savings, inflation protection, remittances, payments and cross-border transfers. Someone buying a dollar-denominated stablecoin to protect savings is solving a different problem from someone opening and funding a leveraged trading account. In North America the case is weaker still, where stablecoins increasingly function as settlement infrastructure and payment rails rather than a retail acquisition pool.

We have not found credible evidence that introducing stablecoins as a funding mechanism materially increases application volume, funded accounts or active traders. Treat the launch as one potential catalyst worth measuring, not as the condition that releases media investment.

